  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-506696 - CO</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">According to the NCHMF, typhoon YAGI made landfall over the border of Quang Ninh - Hai Phong Province at 13.30H UTC+7 on 7 September as a Super Typhoon, equivalent to Hurricane Category 4 on Saffir Simpson Scale. On 8 September typhoon YAGI weakened intoa low-pressure area over the Viet Nam landmass and expected to weaken and gradually dissipate. Typhoon Yagi is reported as the strongest storm in the region in decades, affecting millions of women, men, and children. In addition, accumulated heavy rainfalls pre-, during and after typhoon YAGI have been causing serious floods and landslides in 28 Northern provinces  and cities in Vietnam on 7-10 Sept and are forecast to be worsening in the coming days. The on-going Red River flooding.

The strength and intensity of the typhoon YAGI,and its remnants have left a trail of catastrophic consequences, with millions of families severely affected in coastal as well as mountainous and hard-to-reach areas. 53,000 people displaced and sheltering in flood evacuation centres and more than 101,450 housesand 550 health facilities damaged. Around 19 million people live in the affected provinces. Two million school children are at riskof disruption to learning.

According to reports from local authorities, the following damages have been recorded as of 13:30 on September 11, 2024:
Casualties: 296 people dead and missing (155 dead, 141 missing), specifically: L&#xe0;o Cai: 155 people (53 dead, 102 missing); Cao B&#x1eb1;ng: 52 people in Nguy&#xea;n B&#xec;nh district (29 dead, 23 missing). Y&#xea;n B&#xe1;i: 44 people (40 dead, 04 missing);
Agriculture: 160,851 ha of rice were flooded and damaged; 30,700 ha of crops were flooded and damaged; 16,243 ha of fruit trees were damaged; 1,610 aquaculture cages were damaged and swept away; 1,313 livestock, 793,755 poultry died;
Key infrastructures damaged in Quang Ninh, Hai Phong, Thai Binh, Hai Duong, Hanoi:  widespread power outages and communication lines damaged; 5,305 power poles were broken. As of 11 Sept, power supply and internet services in these provinces are being gradually recovered</narrative>
  </description>
